WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.860
How does test and set

2
00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.200
acquire a lock? It atomically

3
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.940
returns the old lock value

4
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.360
and sets that lock true.

5
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.980
Both parts form one indivisible

6
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.040
shared-memory operation, so two contenders

7
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.780
cannot each observe the same

8
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.220
free lock as their successful

9
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:16.780
acquisition.

10
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.900
Initially the lock is false,

11
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.120
meaning available. P zero executes

12
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.900
test and set and receives

13
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.300
false while the shared lock

14
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.560
becomes true. The false return

15
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.740
is the evidence that this

16
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.900
process acquired the lock, not

17
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.160
a signal that the operation

18
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:32.600
failed.

19
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.800
Now P one attempts the

20
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.200
same atomic operation. It receives

21
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.000
true because the lock was

22
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.480
already held, and the lock

23
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.040
remains true. In the basic

24
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.820
spin loop, a true return

25
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.000
means retry rather than enter

26
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.040
the critical section.

27
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.060
When P zero exits, it

28
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.600
releases the lock by setting

29
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.840
it false according to the

30
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.120
lock protocol. A later test

31
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.480
and set can then return

32
00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.180
false to one contender. Watch

33
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.720
P one become the next

34
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.800
holder only after that successful

35
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.100
free-to-held transition.

36
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.080
Mutual exclusion follows from the

37
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.920
atomic old-value transition: at most

38
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.900
one acquisition can return false

39
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.160
before a release. Progress holds

40
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.420
for a free lock when

41
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.260
an executing contender can acquire

42
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.240
it. Basic test and set

43
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.660
does not by itself provide

44
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.080
a bound on which contender

45
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:22.680
wins next.

46
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.100
P zero might release and

47
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.320
repeatedly reacquire before P one

48
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.160
succeeds. Each individual acquisition is

49
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.800
valid, yet P one can

50
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.320
be overtaken without a fixed

51
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.180
bound. A fair-looking processor schedule

52
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.140
is not the same as

53
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.840
a fair lock acquisition policy.

54
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.620
Atomicity must hold against competing

55
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.380
accesses on other cores too.

56
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.920
Disabling interrupts on one core

57
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.460
does not stop another core

58
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.660
from touching shared memory. The

59
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.820
slide drawings about noninterruption are

60
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.680
a simplified model, not a

61
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.280
general replacement for a multicore

62
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:56.360
atomic instruction.

63
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.640
Spinning can avoid the overhead

64
00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.920
of sleeping when the holder

65
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.580
runs elsewhere and will release

66
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.300
very soon. If the holder

67
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.560
cannot run while a waiter

68
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.240
monopolizes the only core, spinning

69
00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.420
does not make it release

70
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.940
sooner. Compare the expected hold

71
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.600
time with scheduling and switching

72
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.280
costs under the stated assumptions.

73
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.720
A blocking semaphore can still

74
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.600
use a short internal atomic

75
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.980
lock to protect its own

76
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.000
counter and wait list. Blocking

77
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.460
for the resource and briefly

78
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.400
spinning on internal metadata are

79
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.120
different operations. The course excludes

80
00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.620
the more complex bounded-waiting test-and-set

81
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.620
algorithm, so do not substitute

82
00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.000
it for this basic trace.

83
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:35.680
Things to take note of.

84
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.320
False old value acquires, true

85
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.960
old value retries, and release

86
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.140
permits a later winner. Derive

87
00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.240
exclusion from the atomic transition,

88
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.000
but do not infer bounded

89
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.200
waiting or multicore protection from

90
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.040
an unrelated timer or local

91
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:51.780
interrupt rule.
